<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Londinium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Londinium was the Roman city on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, founded around 50 CE at the lowest bridging point of the Thames. Destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>'s revolt in 60 or 61, it was rebuilt and became the largest city and probable capital of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with a forum, basilica, amphitheatre and, from around 200, a defensive wall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/londinium.jpg" alt="Londinium" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fremantleboy, Drallim (translation) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Londinium_400_AD-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Londinium</b>, founded around 50 at the lowest bridging point of the Thames, became the largest city, with a forum and basilica that were among the biggest north of the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
